* Noah's Ark
** The problem
*** b = a.s + e + delta.m
*** Open b - a.[s] and you reveal e
*** e plus the ciphertext leaks the key
*** Mask with [E]: too small leaks, too large breaks rounding
** Why TFHE breaks flooding
*** BGV / BFV: bootstrap or add levels, 14-24 bits each
*** TFHE: q = 2^64, post-bootstrap noise around 2^30
*** The gap is too small, but only by tens of bits
** Switch-n-Squash
*** Just a bootstrap, with keys emitting other parameters
*** (2^64, l) becomes (2^128, L = 4096)
*** Switches modulus and dimension, squashes noise
*** No interaction: the online phase stays one round
*** 241 to 317 ms on an m6i.metal
** Two contributions
*** E as a sum of two uniforms: 2^-2.stat
*** stat = 40 suffices, halving the modulus growth
*** Simulation-based proof, not Renyi games
*** So it composes as a black box
** Two protocols
*** Protocol 1: PRSS, no offline phase, small n-choose-t
*** Protocol 2: offline bit generation, large n-choose-t
*** t < n/3 robust and asynchronous
*** t < n/2 active-with-abort
*** Shamir over Galois rings, via ACD+19
** Paper versus threshold-fhe
*** Normative reference is the NIST doc, not the paper
*** Four decryption modes: flooding and bit decomposition
*** PRSS bound drops the n-choose-t normalisation
*** One AES block, not two; no pow parameter
*** Small means 8192 in code, about 100 in the paper
*** t < n/3 enforced at reconstruction, not at setup
*** n <= 15 from the degree-4 Galois ring
** Status
*** A NIST submission snapshot, not maintained
*** Production is zama-ai/kms
*** Q, L match Table 1; l = 928 does not
